\pard\sb260\sa120\keepn\b\fs26\cf1 First contribution: repository audit\b0\cf0\par
\pard\sa80\par
\pard\f0\fs19\sa70 **Expected output, before any large import:**\par
\pard\sa80\par
\pard\li200\fi-100\fs19\sa35 - Pin a commit/tag for each proposed source project.\par
\pard\li200\fi-100\fs19\sa35 - Record language, frameworks, build instructions, release activity and tests.\par
\pard\li200\fi-100\fs19\sa35 - Verify licenses including transitive libraries, bundled tools and NOTICE obligations.\par
\pard\li200\fi-100\fs19\sa35 - Build and test the proposed base in an isolated environment; capture real results.\par
\pard\li200\fi-100\fs19\sa35 - Map each feature to **implemented upstream / partially implemented / missing**, and separately to **implemented here / unimplemented**.\par
\pard\li200\fi-100\fs19\sa35 - Compare integration costs, security risks, package sizes and maintenance burden.\par
\pard\li200\fi-100\fs19\sa35 - Recommend one base app and extension with a small working integration target.\par
\pard\sa80\par
\pard\f0\fs19\sa70 Use the issue template. Put audit results in a PR; avoid copying upstream trees before approval.\par
\pard\sa80\par
\pard\f0\fs19\sa70 **Current M0 evidence (15 November 2026):** The 17-source triage and two-foundation recommendation have been documented; the original Python HTTP/HTTPS fixture has **14 passing local tests** and a small fixture-only CI workflow. The upstream Chrome/Firefox bundles compile but fail independent TypeScript checks; desktop Gradle bootstrap is network-blocked. See [M0_AUDIT.md](M0_AUDIT.md). These results do not constitute a working downloader.\par
\pard\sa80\par
\pard\sb260\sa120\keepn\b\fs26\cf1 Owner decisions needed before substantial code imports\b0\cf0\par
\pard\sa80\par
\pard\f0\fs19\sa70 1. Product/brand name and trademark checks.\par
\pard\f0\fs19\sa70 2. Intended distribution: open-source, source-available, or proprietary.\par
\pard\f0\fs19\sa70 3. Project license and contributor terms (no LICENSE has been chosen).\par
\pard\f0\fs19\sa70 4. Initial supported OS/browser pair (recommend Windows + Chrome, then Linux/Firefox).\par
\pard\f0\fs19\sa70 5. Whether the initial web app supports uploads, authorized direct URLs, or only marketing.\par
\pard\f0\fs19\sa70 6. Whether any third-party source requires written authorization before downloads.\par
\pard\sa80\par
\pard\sb260\sa120\keepn\b\fs26\cf1 Suggested first runnable demonstration\b0\cf0\par
\pard\sa80\par
\pard\f0\fs19\sa70 With permission to implement, show a Chrome extension sending an **authorized HTTPS file URL** to a desktop app. The app downloads with progress, pause/resume where the server supports range requests, checksum verification, and restart recovery. A short test fixture proves the flow. This validates the hardest shared integration without relying on YouTube compatibility.\par
\pard\sa80\par
\pard\sb260\sa120\keepn\b\fs26\cf1 Common mistakes to avoid\b0\cf0\par
\pard\sa80\par
\pard\li200\fi-100\fs19\sa35 - Calling a planned feature "implemented."\par
\pard\li200\fi-100\fs19\sa35 - Importing AGPL/GPL code into a closed-source product without resolving obligations.\par
\pard\li200\fi-100\fs19\sa35 - Copying user credentials into test fixtures or CI.\par
\pard\li200\fi-100\fs19\sa35 - Building the whole stack before validating one application-extension bridge.\par
\pard\li200\fi-100\fs19\sa35 - Depending on scraping or external network behavior for the entire test suite.\par
\pard\sa80\par
