\pard\sb260\sa120\keepn\b\fs26\cf1 First integration test (proposed)\b0\cf0\par
\pard\sa80\par
\pard\f0\fs19\sa70 The first synthetic [loopback HTTP fixture](../tools/fixtures/README.md) is implemented. From the repository root, run:\par
\pard\sa80\par
\pard\f0\fs19\sa70 ~~~sh\par
\pard\f0\fs19\sa70 python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -v\par
\pard\f0\fs19\sa70 python3 -m tools.fixtures.http_fixture --port 8765\par
\pard\f0\fs19\sa70 ~~~\par
\pard\sa80\par
\pard\f0\fs19\sa70 Use `http://127.0.0.1:8765/file.bin` for Range-aware tests and `/no-range.bin` to test safe full restart. The fixture has **14 passing tests** covering Range/If-Range, redirects, truncated transfers and certificate-validated HTTPS. It supports optional caller-supplied TLS cert/key (see its own README); no desktop downloader has consumed it. The following desktop-to-browser end-to-end test remains proposed.\par
\pard\sa80\par
\pard\f0\fs19\sa70 1. Serve a small openly redistributable test file from a controlled HTTPS endpoint that supports Range requests.\par
\pard\f0\fs19\sa70 2. Download to a user-selected temporary directory.\par
\pard\f0\fs19\sa70 3. Compare SHA-256 hash with the fixture.\par
\pard\f0\fs19\sa70 4. Interrupt the connection and restart/resume.\par
\pard\f0\fs19\sa70 5. Send the same job through the Chrome extension and local bridge.\par
\pard\f0\fs19\sa70 6. Attempt a malicious/unauthorized cross-origin bridge request and verify rejection.\par
\pard\f0\fs19\sa70 7. Record commands, OS/browser versions, pass/fail outcomes and screenshots where useful.\par
\pard\sa80\par
\pard\f0\fs19\sa70 Use synthetic or clearly licensed media; do **not** use arbitrary YouTube videos as default automated tests.\par
\pard\sa80\par
